 "title": "Why Viral Videos Don't Equal More Sales",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "The creator explains that a video with fewer views can sometimes convert into more sales than a highly viral video. He introduces the concept of a content funnel, where half the content gets attention and the other half converts that attention into sales.",
 "key_points": [
  "The creator claims a video with 46,506 views converted infinitely more sales than a video with 11,746,863 views.",
  "Not every video should be made for viral reach.",
  "First video type: For discovery and getting eyes.",
  "Second video type: For sales and getting people to buy.",
  "The content funnel concept: Half your content gives you attention so the other half can convert that attention into sales."
 ],
 "content": "You need a content funnel. #contentcreation #brandowner #advice #viral #contentfunnel Which video do you think got us more sales? This one? Or this one? You got your answer? Well, it was actually the second one. Even though it had 11.7 million less views than the first one, it still converted infinitely more. So why don't we make every video like this? Well, the first video is where people discover you, and the second video is where people buy from you. This is called a content funnel. Half of your content should give you the attention so the other half can convert that attention into sales. And if you want to see more strategies, then hopefully I converted you into a follower. Screen text: Let's goooo! Not your average sweatpants... Why would you strip that man?! Give him his clothes back! Throw! Gray and black baggy sweatpants. 11.7M views. 46k views. For eyes. For sales. Content mix vs Bottom of funnel only. Robert McKeaveney. Co-owner of @wantsandneedsbrand_. TAKE ACTION.",
